# SOLID

Single responsibility comes first. It decides where the code lives.

A class has one reason to change. A JS file has one reason to change too.

- `features/topBar.js` changes when the top bar changes.
- `features/dock.js` changes when the dock changes.
- `preferences/generalPage.js` changes when the General tab changes. Same rule for `panelPage.js` and `dockPage.js`.
- `lib/patch.js` only saves and restores methods. `lib/signals.js` only tracks signal ids.
- `extension.js` only constructs features and calls `enable()` and `disable()`.
- `prefs.js` only adds preferences pages.

A private actor used by one feature stays in that feature's file. When a second feature needs it, move it to its own file. `stylesheet.css` stays the single stylesheet.

```javascript
// ❌ BAD: one file changes for the panel and the dash
export class Shell {
    enable() {
        this._moveClock();
        this._resizeDash();
    }
}

// ✅ GOOD: features/topBar.js
export class TopBar {
    enable() {
        this._moveClock();
    }
}
```

`TopBar` decides what happens to the panel. It uses `PatchSet` instead of implementing a patch stack.

Open/closed: add a feature, or add code to the feature that owns that surface. Leave `PatchSet` and `SignalTracker` generic.

Liskov: an actor subclass must still work wherever the parent works. Call `super._init(...)`. Feature classes share `enable()` and `disable()` but are not a subclass tree. Do not add a base class of empty methods.

Interface segregation: the feature surface is `constructor(extension)`, `enable()`, and `disable()`. `PatchSet` does not track signals. `SignalTracker` does not restore methods.

Dependency inversion: pass the `Extension` in. A feature uses `lib/` helpers and does not import another feature. It does not call `getSettings()` itself. `extension.js` passes `Gio.Settings` in when a task adds settings.
